One important thing to remember

Terraform commands mainly work around three things:

              Terraform
                  │
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
   .tf files   State      AWS
  (desired)   (tfstate)   (actual)
.tf files = what you want
terraform.tfstate = what Terraform knows
AWS = what actually exists

Then plan compares them and apply makes the required changes.
----------------------------------------------
1. terraform init
terraform init
What happens?

Terraform downloads the AWS provider and creates the .terraform directory.

main.tf
   ↓
terraform init
   ↓
AWS provider downloaded

You normally run this first.


2. terraform fmt
terraform fmt    terraform fmt is for .tf files only.

Formats your Terraform code.

For example, poorly formatted:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
ami="ami-xxx"
instance_type="t2.micro"
}

After:

terraform fmt

It becomes:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxx"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

Yes. By default:

terraform fmt

formats the .tf files in the current directory.

For example, if your directory is:

terraform-project/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── provider.tf
└── terraform.tfvars

Running:

terraform fmt

formats the Terraform configuration files in that directory.


What about subdirectories?

Use:

terraform fmt -recursive

Example:

terraform-project/
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── modules/
│   └── ec2/
│       ├── main.tf
│       └── variables.tf
└── environments/
    └── dev/
        └── main.tf

Then:

terraform fmt -recursive

will format the .tf files inside the current directory and its subdirectories.


