11. terraform import

Suppose you manually created an EC2 in AWS:

AWS Console
    ↓
EC2
    ↓
i-0123456789

But Terraform doesn't manage it.

First create a matching resource block:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  # configuration
}

Then:

terraform import aws_instance.web i-0123456789

Now Terraform puts that existing EC2 into its state.

Important:

Existing EC2
     ↓
terraform import
     ↓
terraform.tfstate

import does not mean Terraform creates the EC2. The EC2 already exists.



12. terraform state rm
terraform state rm aws_instance.web

This removes the resource from Terraform state.

⚠️ It does not necessarily delete the EC2 from AWS.

Example:

Before:

Terraform state
      ↓
aws_instance.web
      ↓
EC2

After:

terraform state rm aws_instance.web
Terraform state
      ↓
resource removed

AWS
 ↓
EC2 still exists

This is different from:

terraform destroy

which removes the infrastructure.


Before state rm
AWS:
EC2 ✅

State:
EC2 ✅

main.tf:
EC2 ✅

You run:

terraform state rm aws_instance.app

Now:

AWS:
EC2 ✅

State:
EC2 ❌

main.tf:
EC2 ✅

If you now run:

terraform plan

Terraform sees:

"The EC2 is in main.tf, but I don't have it in my state."

So Terraform will usually plan:

+ aws_instance.app

That means create, not delete.

If you then run:

terraform apply

Terraform may create another EC2.

So:

state rm
   ↓
Terraform forgets EC2
   ↓
apply
   ↓
EC2 in main.tf is seen as new
   ↓
Create new EC2
If you want to delete the AWS resource

Use:

terraform destroy

or remove it from main.tf and then:

terraform apply

Then Terraform can plan:

- aws_instance.app

which means destroy/delete.

Key point: state rm itself doesn't delete the resource, and apply doesn't automatically delete it just because you
removed it from state. The next action depends on what's in your configuration and state



13. terraform state mv

Suppose your resource was:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
}

and you rename it:

resource "aws_instance" "application" {
}

Terraform might think:

DELETE web
CREATE application

Instead, you can move the state:

terraform state mv \
  aws_instance.web \
  aws_instance.application

Now Terraform understands:

This is the same EC2; only the Terraform resource address changed.




What is terraform state pull?
terraform state pull

It means:

"Get the current Terraform state from the configured backend and print the raw state data."

First understand local vs remote state
Case 1: Local state

If you don't configure a backend, Terraform normally stores state locally:

Terraform project
│
├── main.tf
├── .terraform/
└── terraform.tfstate

When you run:

terraform state pull

Terraform reads the current state and prints it.

terraform.tfstate
       ↓
terraform state pull
       ↓
Raw JSON state
Case 2: S3 remote state

In a team/project, you might configure:

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "my-terraform-state"
    key    = "prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "ap-south-1"
  }
}

Now the state is stored in S3, not primarily in your local terraform.tfstate.

AWS S3
│
└── my-terraform-state
       │
       └── prod/terraform.tfstate

When you run:

terraform state pull

Terraform does:

          S3
           │
           │  terraform.tfstate
           ↓
   terraform state pull
           │
           ↓
     Raw state JSON

It downloads/reads the current state and prints it to your terminal.

What does the output look like?

You may see something like:

{
  "version": 4,
  "terraform_version": "1.x.x",
  "resources": [
    {
      "type": "aws_instance",
      "name": "web",
      "instances": [
        {
          "attributes": {
            "id": "i-0123456789",
            "instance_type": "t2.micro"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

This is the raw state representation.


