What does refresh mean?

Refresh means:

"Terraform, check the real infrastructure in AWS and update your state with what actually exists."

So Terraform checks AWS:

AWS
 ↓
"EC2 is t3.micro"
 ↓
Terraform updates state
 ↓
terraform.tfstate now knows t3.micro

But it doesn't change AWS.

terraform apply -refresh-only

You run:

terraform apply -refresh-only

Terraform checks the real infrastructure and asks:

Do you want to update the state?

If you say yes, the state gets updated.

Before:

terraform.tfstate
    ↓
t2.micro

After:

terraform.tfstate
    ↓
t3.micro

AWS was already:

t3.micro

So Terraform didn't change the EC2.


Sure. terraform plan -out is very simple once you understand what a "saved plan" means.

1. Normal terraform plan

Suppose your main.tf says:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxx"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

You run:

terraform plan

Terraform calculates:

main.tf
   +
terraform.tfstate
   ↓
Terraform calculates changes
   ↓
1 EC2 will be created

You see the result on the screen.

But the plan is not saved.

2. terraform plan -out=tfplan

Now run:

terraform plan -out=tfplan

Here:

-out

means:

Save the generated plan to a file.

And:

tfplan

is simply the name of that file.

So:

terraform plan -out=tfplan

means:

"Calculate the plan and save that exact plan in a file called tfplan."

You will now have:

my-project/
│
├── main.tf
├── terraform.tfstate
└── tfplan
3. Then apply the saved plan

Now run:

terraform apply tfplan

Notice we are giving tfplan to apply.

Terraform says:

"Don't calculate a new plan. Use the plan that was already saved in tfplan."

So:

terraform plan -out=tfplan
             ↓
       Save the plan
             ↓
          tfplan
             ↓
terraform apply tfplan
             ↓
        AWS changes
Why is this useful?

Imagine a company has a DevOps team.

Step 1 — Developer creates the plan
terraform plan -out=tfplan

Terraform says:

Plan:

+ aws_instance.web
+ aws_security_group.web

Plan: 2 to add

The team can review this.

They might say:

"Okay, this plan looks correct."

Step 2 — Apply that exact plan
terraform apply tfplan

Terraform executes the saved plan.

Saved plan
    ↓
terraform apply tfplan
    ↓
AWS
    ↓
EC2 + Security Group
Why not just do this?

You might ask:

terraform plan

then:

terraform apply

What's the difference?

With:

terraform apply

Terraform calculates a plan again before applying.

So:

terraform plan
      ↓
Plan A
      ↓
time passes
      ↓
terraform apply
      ↓
Terraform calculates another plan
      ↓
Plan B
      ↓
Apply Plan B

But with:

terraform plan -out=tfplan

then:

terraform apply tfplan

you are saying:

terraform plan -out=tfplan
      ↓
Plan A saved
      ↓
Review Plan A
      ↓
terraform apply tfplan
      ↓
Apply Plan A

That's why it is useful in CI/CD pipelines, where one stage generates a plan and another approved stage applies it.

Remember this
terraform plan

➡️ Show me the plan

terraform plan -out=tfplan

➡️ Create the plan AND save it

terraform apply tfplan

➡️ Apply the saved plan

And tfplan is not the state file.

terraform.tfstate  → Terraform's state/record

tfplan              → Saved proposed changes

They are two completely different files.

